WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.320
How do producers and consumers

2
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.740
share a bounded buffer? We

3
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.340
need to coordinate the available

4
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.440
slots, the available items and

5
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.840
safe access to the buffer

6
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.200
structure. A single lock does

7
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.900
not express all three of

8
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.680
those requirements.

9
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.400
Use three slots, with empty

10
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.160
initially three, full initially zero

11
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.160
and mutex initially one. Empty

12
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.300
permits producers to reserve capacity,

13
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.720
full permits consumers to reserve

14
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.100
items, and mutex protects shared

15
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.300
insertion or removal data. The

16
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.580
values have different meanings.

17
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.140
A producer prepares an item,

18
00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.360
waits on empty, and then

19
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.320
waits on mutex. Its capacity

20
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.700
reservation changes empty from three

21
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.440
to two before the item

22
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.880
is physically inserted. Reserving a

23
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.440
slot and filling that slot

24
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.780
are separate steps in the

25
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:51.080
trace.

26
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.880
Inside the protected region, insert

27
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.920
item A and update the

28
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.820
shared buffer metadata. Release mutex,

29
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.360
then signal full so an

30
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.180
item becomes available for consumption.

31
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.600
At the completed operation boundary,

32
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.500
the buffer has one item,

33
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.680
empty is two and full

34
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:09.220
is one.

35
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.680
A consumer first waits on

36
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.460
full and then on mutex.

37
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.360
It reserves an available item

38
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.100
before removing it under mutual

39
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.720
exclusion. After removal it releases

40
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.220
mutex and signals empty, returning

41
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.180
capacity to the producer side.

42
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.620
Fill the remaining example with

43
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.260
three produced items A, B

44
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.180
and C. When every slot

45
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.420
is occupied and there is

46
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.600
no free capacity, an additional

47
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.280
producer must wait for a

48
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.460
consumer to release space. It

49
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.760
must not hold the buffer

50
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.380
mutex while waiting for that

51
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:41.880
capacity.

52
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.220
The consumer has the dual

53
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.300
rule: if no item is

54
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.980
available, wait on full before

55
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.080
taking mutex. If it instead

56
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.740
takes mutex first and then

57
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.140
blocks on an empty buffer,

58
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.880
the producer cannot acquire mutex

59
00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.640
to insert an item. The

60
00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.240
two processes can then wait

61
00:01:59.240 --> 00:01:59.940
on each other.

62
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.820
The same mistake on a

63
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:02.940
full buffer can leave a

64
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.900
producer holding mutex while waiting

65
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.220
for empty. A consumer needs

66
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.640
that mutex to remove an

67
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.980
item and create capacity. This

68
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.820
ordering defect is not repaired

69
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.580
merely by adding another processor.

70
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.360
Track physical items and outstanding

71
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.680
reservations separately. Empty plus full

72
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.860
equals capacity at simple quiescent

73
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.160
boundaries, but not necessarily between

74
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.240
a wait and its matching

75
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.300
completion signal, especially in the

76
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.180
signed blocking representation. An invariant

77
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.040
must account for in-flight operations

78
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.900
rather than pretending each instruction

79
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.060
is a completed transaction.

80
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.860
Things to take note of.

81
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.400
Producers wait for capacity before

82
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.760
mutex; consumers wait for an

83
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.140
item before mutex. Update buffer

84
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.080
data inside the protected region,

85
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.480
signal the opposite resource after

86
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.160
completing the change, and explain

87
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.980
the specific deadlock caused by

88
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.220
swapping those waits.
